          "content": [
            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Let \\\\( N \\\\) be a positive integer. Two persons play the following game. The first player writes a list of positive integers not greater than \\\\( 25 \\\\), not necessarily different, such that their sum is at least \\\\( 200 \\\\). The second player wins if he can select some of these numbers so that their sum \\\\( S \\\\) satisfies the condition \\\\( 200-N \\\\leq S \\\\leq 200+N \\\\). What is the smallest value of \\\\( N \\\\) for which the second player has a winning strategy?\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"baltic_mo\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"The smallest value is \\\\(\\\\boxed{11}\\\\).\\n\\nFirst, \\\\(N=10\\\\) is not sufficient. Let the first player write four \\\\(25\\\\)'s, four \\\\(22\\\\)'s, and one \\\\(23\\\\). Their total is\\n\\\\[\\n4\\\\cdot25+4\\\\cdot22+23=211.\\n\\\\]\\nEvery proper subset omits a number at least \\\\(22\\\\), so its sum is at most \\\\(211-22=189\\\\); the full set has sum \\\\(211\\\\). Hence no subset sum lies in \\\\([190,210]\\\\). Thus \\\\(N\\\\ge 11\\\\).\\n\\nNow consider any permitted list whose total is at least \\\\(200\\\\). Among all subsets having sum at least \\\\(189\\\\), choose one with the smallest sum, say \\\\(s\\\\). Such a subset exists because the whole list has sum at least \\\\(200\\\\).\\n\\nFor every selected number \\\\(a\\\\), minimality gives \\\\(s-a<189\\\\). Since \\\\(a\\\\le25\\\\), it follows that\\n\\\\[\\ns<189+a\\\\le214,\\n\\\\]\\nso, as \\\\(s\\\\) is integral, \\\\(189\\\\le s\\\\le213\\\\).\\n\\nIt remains to exclude \\\\(s=212\\\\) and \\\\(s=213\\\\). If \\\\(s=212\\\\), then \\\\(s-a<189\\\\) implies \\\\(a>23\\\\), so every selected number is \\\\(24\\\\) or \\\\(25\\\\). At most eight such numbers sum to at most \\\\(200\\\\), while at least nine sum to at least \\\\(216\\\\), making sum \\\\(212\\\\) impossible. If \\\\(s=213\\\\), then every selected number satisfies \\\\(a>24\\\\), so all are \\\\(25\\\\), whose sum cannot be \\\\(213\\\\).\\n\\nTherefore \\\\(189\\\\le s\\\\le211\\\\), which is exactly \\\\(200-11\\\\le s\\\\le200+11\\\\). Hence \\\\(N=11\\\\) is sufficient, and the lower-bound construction proves it is minimal.\"}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
